     "title": "Suno shares plans to combat spammy AI music",
     "url": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/976289/suno-ai-music-spam-watermark",
     "time": "08-07 01:39",
     "ts": 1786037983,
     "summary": "Suno announced plans to implement a new watermarking technology and download policy to limit the spread of spammy AI tracks and increase transparency. In a leng",
     "source": "The Verge AI",
     "content": "Suno announced plans to implement a new watermarking technology and download policy to limit the spread of spammy AI tracks and increase transparency. In a lengthy blog post, CEO and co-founder Mikey Shulman laid out the company’s principles and the next steps for the company as it seeks legitimacy.\nSuno shares plans to combat spammy AI music\nThe company is changing its download policy and rolling out new watermarking technology.\nThe company is changing its download policy and rolling out new watermarking technology.\nThe company is rolling out new transparency tools, along with new watermarking and fingerprinting tech. Shulman says it aligns with “emerging industry standards” that will make it easier to identify Suno-generated content. He also says the company is aiming to partner with “distribution platforms on combatting fraud and misuse.”\nLast year, the company also announced it would change its download policy following a settlement with Warner Music Group (WMG), and in the wake of a similar settlement between WMG and Udio that ended downloads of outputs from that platform entirely. The company hasn’t offered specifics yet, but at the time it announced the plan last year, it said that downloads would be limited to paying subscribers, and users would be limited to a certain number of downloads each month.\nShulman’s post repeatedly mentions the company’s dedication to transparency, respect for rights holders, and the importance of human-made art. But he’s also careful to say it’s not Suno’s place to “pass judgment” on the value of individual pieces of art, and that it’s ultimately up to artists and platforms to decide whether they want to disclose the use of AI tools.\nSuno didn’t immediately respond to a request for specifics about the new policies and technology.\nMore in: All the latest in AI ‘music’\nMost Popular\n- Ford’s first ultra-cheap EV is called Fathom, a full-featured truck for $28,350\n- AI bots started a religion — humans immediately followed\n- The messy politics behind Google’s big AI shakeup\n- Jony Ive’s first OpenAI gadget is reportedly a hockey puck-sized smart speaker\n- GTA VI is getting an ‘extended look’ at the end of August",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "Suno公布打击垃圾AI音乐的计划",
     "content_zh": "AI音乐生成平台Suno宣布将推出新水印技术、调整下载政策并上线透明度工具，以遏制垃圾AI音乐的传播、提升内容可追溯性。CEO Mikey Shulman表示相关举措符合新兴行业标准，能便于识别Suno生成内容，同时公司正寻求与分发平台合作打击欺诈与滥用行为。此前Suno已因与华纳音乐集团（WMG）达成和解，以及WMG与竞品Udio和解后禁止后者输出内容下载，宣布将调整下载政策，具体细则尚未公布。"
