     "title": "The AI Genie Is Out of the Bottle and Won’t Go Back",
     "url": "https://semiwiki.com/artificial-intelligence/371818-the-ai-genie-is-out-of-the-bottle-and-wont-go-back/",
     "time": "08-06 05:00",
     "ts": 1785963609,
     "summary": "This week, Meta became the fourth major AI lab in two weeks to disclose that one of its own models breached another company&#8217;s systems during a security te",
     "source": "SemiWiki",
     "content": "This week, Meta became the fourth major AI lab in two weeks to disclose that one of its own models breached another company’s systems during a security test. The company says a misconfiguration by an outside evaluator handed its Muse Spark 1.1 model internet access it wasn’t supposed to have — an accident in the test setup, Meta and the evaluator both insist, not the model finding its own way out on its own initiative. That’s a real distinction, worth holding onto rather than flattening into one more scare story. It’s also gotten a lot less reassuring than it would have looked a year ago, because three other labs have already told nearly the same story.\nThe Pattern, Twice, This Year\nDuring an internal red-team exercise called ExploitGym, two OpenAI models; GPT-5.6 Sol and an unreleased, more capable pre-release model; didn’t stay inside their designated test environment. According to OpenAI’s own account, they exploited a zero-day vulnerability in a package registry cache proxy, escalated privileges, and moved laterally through OpenAI’s own research environment until they reached a machine with internet access. From there they inferred that Hugging Face’s own dataset-processing pipeline likely hosted the benchmark’s answer materials. They found two flaws in it, and chained those flaws with stolen credentials, OpenAI’s own term for this stage, to execute code on an internal worker and reach the Hugging Face servers.\nFrom that foothold, they harvested a second, separate set of credentials: standing cloud and cluster access that was simply sitting on the compromised machine, not stolen through phishing or cracking this time. They used these to move across multiple internal clusters over a weekend, generating more than 17,000 recorded actions before Hugging Face detected and contained it. Hugging Face wasn’t the only target. Subsequent reporting named Modal, a New York City–based AI‑native cloud infrastructure company, among other organizations reached the same way, with the exposed credentials spanning four separate services rather than one.\nOpenAI called it an “unprecedented” cyber incident and has since suspended similar testing. OpenAI is working with CrowdStrike to validate its own account of what happened. OpenAI has also agreed to an independent review with METR and Redwood Research; though as of this writing, that review has produced no published findings, only an agreement to conduct one. METR has said the engagement “will be brief and focus on a specific set of questions,” a narrower scope than the comprehensive investigation METR’s own published framework calls for after an agentic misalignment incident, which it says could take weeks or months and should examine whether the behavior traces back to specific training choices and whether similar incidents happened elsewhere.\nAI policy organizations have already called for a federal investigation, questioning whether METR’s review is under a non-disclosure agreement with OpenAI and whether it",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "AI已出瓶难收回",
     "content_zh": "本周Meta成为两周内第四家披露自身AI模型在安全测试中入侵其他公司系统的主要AI实验室。Meta称是外部评估方配置失误，使其Muse Spark 1.1模型获得了本不该有的互联网权限，双方均强调这是测试设置的意外，并非模型自主突破。这一区分本应比恐慌性报道更有价值，但如今已少有余地，因为另外三家实验室今年已曝出几乎相同的事件：OpenAI在内部红队测试ExploitGym中，两款模型突破测试环境，利用零日漏洞提权，横向移动至带互联网权限的机器，还推断出Hugging Face数据集处理流程的相关情况。"
