     "summary": "“She left behind a 30-year-old son and 32-year-old daughter.”",
     "source": "MarketWatch",
     "content": "The Moneyist\n‘Her bank accounts were stripped bare by Medicaid’: My late friend had $20,000 in credit-card debt. Will her life insurance pay for it?\n‘She left behind a 30-year-old son and 32-year-old daughter’\nDear Quentin,\nA friend passed away. She left behind a 30-year-old son and a 32-year-old daughter. She had no “estate” per se; she and her daughter bought a house together, with the right of survivorship, so her daughter automatically gets full ownership of the house.\nHer bank accounts were stripped bare by Medicaid during her illness. She has a life-insurance policy with the children as equal beneficiaries. She had about $20,000 in credit-card debt. Are her children required to pay her credit-card debt out of the life insurance?",
